Out of memory, the length Vim keeps for its cached line can be left impossible:
u_save_line then asked malloc for 0xffc7d8b8 bytes, and Amix's malloc, which has
no ceiling, shrank the heap under live data (a 57 MB edit under a 32 Mbyte data
limit, on a 68060).  The undo save now fails instead.  Why the length goes bad
is Vim's own and is left to upstream.

--- a/src/undo.c
+++ b/src/undo.c
@@ -369,6 +369,15 @@
     {
 	// This uses the length in the memline, thus text properties are
 	// included.
+#ifdef __amix__
+	// Out of memory can leave the cached length impossible (seen: just
+	// under 4 Gbyte).  Fail the undo save rather than ask malloc for it.
+	if (curbuf->b_ml.ml_line_len < 0 || curbuf->b_ml.ml_line_len > MAXCOL)
+	{
+	    ul->ul_line = NULL;
+	    return FAIL;
+	}
+#endif
 	ul->ul_len = curbuf->b_ml.ml_line_len;
 	ul->ul_line = vim_memsave(line, ul->ul_len);
     }
